        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Zoroaster</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">dates disputed: c. 1000 BCE, or 7th–6th century BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">Place unknown, probably in the east of Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a>; dated by many scholars to around 1000 BCE and by Zoroastrian tradition to the 7th and 6th centuries BCE; the son of Pourushaspa of the Spitama family and Dugdōw</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">At the age of 77 years and 40 days; most commonly said to have been murdered at an altar by a priest of the old religion named Brādrēs, while other traditions name a Turanian soldier or old age</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Priest, preacher and poet-prophet who, in Zoroastrian tradition, received a revelation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>; scholars describe him as an Iranian religious reformer</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the revelation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>; the contrast of asha (truth, order) and druj (deception); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gathas">Gathas</a>; the patronage of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishtaspa">Vishtaspa</a>; the stress on free will and on good thoughts, words and deeds; the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a></span></div>
