    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Little is known of Zoroaster beyond a few early texts, and by modern standards of historiography nothing can place him in a fixed period.</li><li>According to Zoroastrian tradition, he received a revelation at about 30, taught the choice between asha and druj, and gained the patronage of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishtaspa">Vishtaspa</a>.</li><li>His teaching stresses free will and ethical responsibility, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> later became the most prominent religion of Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Greek writers placed Zoroaster thousands of years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> in 480 BCE, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> wrote that there had been two Zoroasters.</p></aside>
    </section>

